    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
          <title>Derivation of the Sine Rule:</title>
          <figure>
            <caption>The sine rule</caption>
            <image width="50%">
                <shortdescription>Triangle ABC with altitude h from C to side AB, creating two right triangles.</shortdescription>
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}
                       % Triangle vertices
                        \coordinate (A) at (0, 0);
                        \coordinate (B) at (4, 0);
                        \coordinate (C) at (2.8, 1.8);
                        \coordinate (D) at (2.8, 0);

                        % Draw triangle
                        \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

                        % Draw altitude
                        \draw[dashed, blue!70!black] (C) -- (D);
                        \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (D) -- (C);

                        % Draw right angle marker
                        \draw[blue!70!black] (D) -- ($(D) + (0.2, 0)$) -- ($(D) + (0.2, 0.2)$) -- ($(D) + (0, 0.2)$);

                        % Label vertices
                        \node[below left, font=\large] at (A) {$A$};
                        \node[below right, font=\large] at (B) {$B$};
                        \node[above, font=\large] at (C) {$C$};
                        \node[below, font=\small, blue!70!black] at (D) {$D$};

                        % Label angles
                        \node[right, font=\small] at (0.4, 0.15) {$A$};
                        \node[left, font=\small] at (3.6, 0.15) {$B$};

                        % Label sides
                        \node[right, font=\small] at (3.4, 0.9) {$b$};
                        \node[left, font=\small] at (1.4, 0.9) {$a$};
                        \node[below, font=\small] at (2, -0.25) {$c$};

                        % Label altitude
                        \node[right, font=\small, blue!70!black] at (2.9, 0.9) {$h$}; 
                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
          </figure>
           <p>
             Consider triangle <m>ABC</m> (above) with sides <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, <m>c</m> opposite to angles <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m> respectively. Draw an altitude (height) <m>h</m> from vertex <m>C</m> perpendicular to side <m>AB</m>. 
             This altitude divides the triangle into two right triangles.
          </p>

        <p>
            In the left right triangle (containing angle <m>A</m>): <m>\sin A = \dfrac{h}{b}</m>, so <m>h = b\sin A</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            In the right right triangle (containing angle <m>B</m>): <m>\sin B = \dfrac{h}{a}</m>, so <m>h = a\sin B</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Since both expressions equal <m>h</m>:
            <md><mrow>b\sin A = a\sin B</mrow></md>
        </p>

        <p>
            Rearranging:
            <md><mrow>\frac{a}{\sin A} = \frac{b}{\sin B}</mrow></md>
        </p>

        <p>
             By drawing an altitude from a different vertex, 
             we can show that <m>\dfrac{b}{\sin B} = \dfrac{c}{\sin C}</m> as well, giving us the complete sine rule:
              <md>
                 <mrow>\frac{a}{\sin A} = \frac{b}{\sin B} = \frac{c}{\sin C}</mrow>
             </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>The sine rule is powerful when you know either two angles and one opposite side, or two sides and a non-included angle; it lets you solve triangles that cannot be handled with right-triangle trigonometry alone.</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>Always check the value of <m>\sin B</m>: if it is greater than 1, the combination of given side and angle is impossible, and if it is between 0 and 1 there may still be two possible angles in the ambiguous case.</p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
              <p>
                 <title>Derivation of Cosine rule</title>
              </p>
        <p>
            Consider triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> with sides<m>a = BC</m>, <m>b = AC</m>, and <m>c = AB</m>.
            Let angle <m>C</m> be the included angle between sides<m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.
        </p>
            <figure>
                <caption>The cosine rule</caption>
                <image width="60%">
                    <shortdescription>Triangle used to derive cosine rule.</shortdescription>
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.8]
                            % Triangle vertices
                            \coordinate (A) at (0, 0);
                            \coordinate (B) at (4, 0);
                            \coordinate (C) at (2.8, 1.8);
                            \coordinate (D) at (2.8, 0);
    
                            % Draw triangle
                            \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;
    
                            % Draw altitude
                            \draw[dashed, blue!70!black] (C) -- (D);
                            \draw[blue!70!black, thick] (D) -- (C);
    
                            % Draw right angle marker
                            \draw[blue!70!black] (D) -- ($(D) + (0.2, 0)$) -- ($(D) + (0.2, 0.2)$) -- ($(D) + (0, 0.2)$);
    
                            % Label vertices
                            \node[below left, font=\large] at (A) {$A$};
                            \node[below right, font=\large] at (B) {$B$};
                            \node[above, font=\large] at (C) {$C$};
                            \node[below, font=\small, blue!70!black] at (D) {$D$};
    
                            % Label angles
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (0.4, 0.15) {$A$};
                            \node[left, font=\small] at (3.6, 0.15) {$B$};
    
                            % Label sides
                            \node[right, font=\small] at (3.4, 0.9) {$b$};
                            \node[left, font=\small] at (1.4, 0.9) {$a$};
                            \node[below, font=\small] at (2, -0.25) {$c$};
                            \node[below, font=\small] at (2.8, -0.25) {$x$};
                            \node[below, font=\small] at (3.6, -0.25) {$c-x$};
                            % Label altitude
                            \node[left, font=\small, blue!70!black] at (2.8, 1.8) {$h$};

                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        <p>
            Drop a perpendicular from <m>A</m> to side <m>CB</m>, meeting it at point <m>D</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            In right triangle <m>\triangle ACD</m>:
            <md>
               CD = b\cos C 
            </md>
             and 
                 <md>
                     AD = b\sin C.
                 </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Since the entire side <m>CB</m> has length <m>a</m>,
            the segment <m>DB</m> is:
        </p>
        <p>
             <md>
                 DB = a - b\cos C.
             </md>
        </p>
          <p>
             Now consider right triangle <m>\triangle ADB</m>.By the Pythagorean Theorem,
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
                 c^2 = AD^2 + DB^2.
             </md>
          </p>
        <p>
            Substituting the expressions for <m>AD</m> and <m>DB</m> gives:
        </p>
      <p>
         <md>
            c^2 = (b\sin C)^2 + (a - b\cos C)^2.
        </md>
      </p>
    
        <p>
            Expanding the square:
        </p>
        <p>
             <md>
                 c^2 = b^2\sin^2 C + a^2 - 2ab\cos C + b^2\cos^2 C.
            </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Rearranging terms,
        </p>
        <p>
             <md>
                  c^2 = a^2 + b^2(\sin^2 C + \cos^2 C) - 2ab\cos C.
             </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Using the Pythagorean identity we obtain:
        </p>
         <p>
              <md>
                 c^2 = a^2 + b^2(1) - 2ab\cos C.
              </md>
        </p>
        <p>
            Therefore,
        </p>
        <p>
           <md>
               c^2 = a^2 + b^2 - 2ab\cos C.
            </md> 
        </p>
       
    </insight>

